4-42608bbe9957" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399981 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation $x+2y=4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{2}x+2)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+4 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={2}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4-15 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-11<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}-2x+3=0.$\n\nHence, we conclude that the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $x+2y=4$ at any point.\n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/40/400b997f9f1e83d6d2dd6c34b68a01ad4de5b352e40de2401eb516ca181f3163.png)" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399982 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399983 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399982 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399984 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399982 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399985 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399982 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399986 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399982 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399987 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399982 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399982 1077 "selection" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399982 1079 #edb/ref 17592186399986 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399982 1080 #uuid "d47ad7cb-b24b-5fa1-931d-75679414940e" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399983 1081 "$1$ only" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399983 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186399983 1084 