as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400134 1072 #uuid "df6cb91b-a536-5526-9dd6-80b470cc8a61" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400134 1074 "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+3 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\le 1+3 \\\\ 2 & \\le y\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $2\\le y\\le 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $2\\le y\\le 4.$" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400136 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400137 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400138 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400139 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400135 1075 #edb/ref 17592186400140 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400135 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400135 1077 "selection" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400135 1079 #edb/ref 17592186400139 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [17592186400135 1080 #uuid "ef6815f3-6a4d-5946-a00b-a698526a94b2" 13194139533480 17592186045585 true] [175921